// Copyright (C) Shielded Technologies
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
//
// The Backgammon table: two seats, one flat pot, Yacht's dust dice -- the ledger-9 build, whose
// every roll hashes a secret from EACH player and nothing of the operator's (backgammon-dice).
//
// One deployment per game, like the Yacht table (table.compact), and advertised the same way --
// by a second instance of the unchanged Lobby. The winner takes the pot less a 1% rake. No
// doubling cube and no gammons: a win is a win.
//
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. WHAT THE CHAIN ENFORCES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// ENFORCED, per checker (backgammon-board.compact): there is a checker of yours on the point it
// leaves; a checker on the bar enters before anything else moves; the landing point does not
// hold two or more of the opponent's; a single opponent checker there is hit to the bar;
// bearing off only once all fifteen are home; a die larger than the point bears off only from
// the highest occupied point; the dice used are the dice rolled (doubles: four of one value).
//
// NOT ENFORCED: that a ply plays AS MANY dice as it can, and that when only one of two can be
// played it is the larger. Deciding either needs a search over every order the dice could be
// played in, which is too large for a circuit. The client offers only full legal plies and the
// chain-only verifier reports any ply that broke the rule. `applyPlyStrict` enforces the cheap
// half -- a die left unplayed must be unplayable on the final board -- which closes "just pass".
//
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. A PLY IS ONE TRANSACTION, AND THE OPERATOR IS NOT IN IT
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//     filling  two or more have joined     ->  start(now)              anyone: seats the two earliest
//     stage 0  both seats owe the opening  ->  reveal(seat, now)       each seat's share, any order
//              then the higher opening die ->  openingMove(ply, now)   ply 0, with the opening dice
//     stage 1  the player to move          ->  move(ply, now)          ply 1 on
//
// Nobody answers a roll. A ply's dice hash the mover's own secret for that ply and the secret the
// previous move revealed (backgammon-dice.compact), so the mover computes its roll the moment the
// opponent's move lands, and its move reveals the secret that proves it. The dice of ply t >= 1
// are PRIVATE to the mover until that move: nobody else holds its secret. The opening is public
// once both shares are revealed.
//
// A pass (`count == 0`) is a move like any other and reveals its secret too: the other seat's
// next roll hashes it. A shut-out player therefore still sends its pass -- the website sends a
// forced one by itself -- because skipping it would leave the next roll hashing only the mover's
// own secret (reverted 2026-09-29, db2e94d).
//
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. TIME
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Declared-and-sandwiched, as table.compact section 5: `join`, `start`, `reveal`, `openingMove`
// and `move` declare `now`, pinned within `timeSlackSecs()` of block time. Every clock a call
// starts is stamped a slack AHEAD -- `deadline = now + timeSlackSecs() + moveTimeoutSecs` -- so
// the seat it is for has AT LEAST `moveTimeoutSecs` from the block the call landed in, whatever time the
// caller declared (security-review C1: a deadline of `now + moveTimeoutSecs` would let the caller
// start the other seat's clock in the past). Everything else compares block time against the
// stored `deadline` with a kernel predicate:
//
//     eliminate (timeout)    the seat that owes -- the one to move, or a share not yet revealed
//                            while the other's is -- past `deadline`          -> the other wins
//     abortTable (opening)   neither share revealed past deadline + tableTimeoutSecs -> refunds
//     abortTable (filling)   a table timeout after the latest join            -> slots refunded
//
// Both reveals stamp `deadline` BLINDLY (they read nothing the other writes, so they can land in
// one block): the later one's stamp stands, and the starter's clock runs from the second share.
//
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. THE RANDOMNESS, AND WHAT IS LEFT
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Every roll takes a secret from each player, forced from the key it committed at join, and
// nothing from the operator (backgammon-dice.compact). So no one -- player, operator, or any two
// of them together -- can choose a roll, and no one but the mover can compute one before the move
// that plays it. Trying keys at join buys nothing: every roll also needs the other player's
// secret, which is not known then, and a join reveals only its key's commitment.
//
// What is left is the order of revealing, and it only ever hurts the one who uses it. The later
// of the two opening shares sees the opening first; a mover sees its roll before it moves. Either
// can decline to act, and declining loses the game on the clock (`eliminate`). Neither can change
// anything it reveals.
//
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 6. JOINS DO NOT CONFLICT
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A proof is bound to every ledger value its transaction READ, and the node refuses it if any of
// them moved (per read, not per write -- docs/order-independent-join.md). So a join that read a
// shared seat counter could never land in the same block as another join. Here a join claims one
// of `slotCount()` pre-inserted SLOTS, at random (the client picks a free one), and reads nothing
// but `phase`, constants and its own slot: two joins to different slots touch nothing in common
// and both land. Two to the same slot both read it, so exactly one lands and the other is refused
// before inclusion, its stake untouched -- the read is the guard, and it is mandatory: without it
// both would land and one player's identity would silently overwrite the other's.
//
// Nothing shared is counted while the table fills -- no seat counter, no pot, no set of keys --
// because counting is a read-modify-write. `start` -- anyone may send it; the operator does --
// reads every slot, seats the two EARLIEST joiners (by declared join time, slot breaking ties) as
// seats 0 and 1, refunds anyone else who joined in the same moment and sets the pot.
//
// Consequences, stated: the same secret may sit in two slots (seated twice, it plays itself for
// its own stake -- the old one-seat-per-key set was the growing structure that made concurrent
// joins run out of gas, docs/order-independent-join.md "Probe results"); and a join declares its
// time within `timeSlackSecs()` of block time, so among joiners minutes apart a later one can
// claim to be up to that much earlier. Only a third joiner, before the start, can lose a seat to
// that -- and it is refunded in full.
//
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 5. k, AND THE DEPLOY
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Eight exported circuits: join, start, reveal, openingMove, move, eliminate, settle, abortTable.
// With no VRF a move proves no curve arithmetic; `move` is k=15 because the opening's hash lives
// in `openingMove`, used once a game. Measured values: cli/tools/circuit-k.mjs.

pragma language_version >= 0.26;

import CompactStandardLibrary;

include "dice-core";

include "policy-core";

include "backgammon-dice";

include "backgammon-board";

// ===========================================================================================
// Constants
// ===========================================================================================

/** Slack on a declared `now`, in seconds. Identical to the Yacht table's (security-review C1). */
pure circuit timeSlackSecs(): Uint<64> {
  return 120;
}

/** The table timeout must strictly exceed `timeSlackSecs() * timeoutSlackFactor()` = 240 s. */
pure circuit timeoutSlackFactor(): Uint<64> {
  return 2;
}

/** The shortest move clock a table may seal: time to prove and land a move. */
pure circuit minMoveSecs(): Uint<64> {
  return 120;
}

/** Smallest stake: the 1% rake is never zero. */
pure circuit minTier(): Uint<64> {
  return 100;
}

/** Largest stake, 10^15, so `tier * 2` stays far inside `Uint<64>`. */
pure circuit maxTier(): Uint<64> {
  return 1000000000000000;
}

/**
 * Seat slots a join may claim (section 6). More than the two seats so that two players joining
 * at once collide only one time in eight; the start seats two and refunds the rest. Must match
 * the `Vector<8, SlotInfo>` of `allSlots`.
 */
export pure circuit slotCount(): Uint<8> {
  return 8;
}

/** "Nobody": the value of `winner` until someone has won. */
export pure circuit noWinner(): Uint<8> {
  return 2;
}

/** Both seats owe their share of the opening; then the starter owes ply 0. */
export pure circuit stageOpening(): Uint<8> {
  return 0;
}

/** The player to move owes its move (ply 1 on). */
export pure circuit stageMove(): Uint<8> {
  return 1;
}

export enum BgPhase {
  filling,
  playing,
  decided,
  settled,
  aborted,
}

/** Both sides, each from its own point of view (see backgammon-board.compact). */
export struct Board {
  s0: Vector<26, Uint<8>>,
  s1: Vector<26, Uint<8>>,
}

/** Written once per seat, by the start. Pre-inserted as zeros so every lookup is total. */
export struct SeatIdentity {
  addr: UserAddress,
  keyCommit: Bytes<32>,
}

// ===========================================================================================
// Ledger
// ===========================================================================================

/** Unique per table: the domain separator on every roll and entropy hash. */
export sealed ledger tableId: Bytes<32>;

/** Stake per seat. The pot holds exactly `tier * seatCount`. */
export sealed ledger tier: Uint<64>;

export sealed ledger rakeAddress: UserAddress;

/** How long the seat that owes has, from the call that made it owe, before it can be eliminated. */
export sealed ledger moveTimeoutSecs: Uint<64>;

/** The fill clock, and the grace before a table nobody opened is refunded. See section 3. */
export sealed ledger tableTimeoutSecs: Uint<64>;

/** `inviteCommitment(code)` on a private table; zeros on a public one. */
export sealed ledger inviteHash: Bytes<32>;

export ledger phase: BgPhase;

/** 0 while filling (nothing is counted then -- section 6), 2 from the start. */
export ledger seatCount: Uint<8>;

/** The seat whose ply it is, from ply 1. At the opening it is the higher die's, read from the shares. */
export ledger toMove: Uint<8>;

/** `stageOpening()` or `stageMove()`. */
export ledger stage: Uint<8>;

/** Plies played so far. 0 until the opening ply has been played. */
export ledger turn: Uint<16>;

/**
 * The dice of the last ply PLAYED -- written by its move, which is where they become public.
 * Zeros before ply 0 (the opening pair follows from the two shares; see `bgOpeningDice`).
 */
export ledger dice: BgDice;

export ledger board: Board;

/** The last ply played, verbatim, so a client and the verifier can read what happened. */
export ledger lastPly: Ply;

/** See section 3. */
export ledger deadline: Uint<64>;

export ledger pot: Uint<64>;

/** The winning seat once decided; `noWinner()` before. */
export ledger winner: Uint<8>;

/** How the table ended, as one hash. Zeros until `settle` or `abortTable`. */
export ledger finalDigest: Bytes<32>;

export ledger seatIdentity: Map<Uint<8>, SeatIdentity>;

/** Each seat's share of the opening, revealed after the start; zeros until then. */
export ledger openingShare: Map<Uint<8>, Bytes<32>>;

/** The roll secret the last move revealed; the next roll hashes it. Zeros before ply 0. */
export ledger lastSecret: Bytes<32>;

/** One slot of a filling table (section 6): who joined it, and when it declared it joined. */
export struct BgSlot {
  addr: UserAddress,
  keyCommit: Bytes<32>,
  joinedAt: Uint<64>,
}

/** The filling table's slots. ONE map of records, not a map per field: see the constructor. */
export ledger joinSlots: Map<Uint<8>, BgSlot>;

/** Sink for the transaction padding. See table.compact section 3. */
export ledger padStore: Bytes<4096>;

pure circuit paddingConstant(): Bytes<4096> {
  return pad(4096, "dust-dice:bg:v1:pad");
}

// ===========================================================================================
// Witnesses -- a subset of the Yacht table's (src/table-witnesses.ts serves both)
// ===========================================================================================

/** The seated player's secret `sk`. `join` commits to it; `reveal` and `move` prove it. */
witness playerEntropySecret(): Bytes<32>;

/** A private table's invite code. Zeros otherwise. */
witness inviteCode(): Bytes<32>;

// ===========================================================================================
// Helpers
// ===========================================================================================

/** The closing certificate. See table.compact's `FinalEvent`; this one also binds the board. */
export struct BgFinalEvent {
  domain: Bytes<32>,
  tableId: Bytes<32>,
  ending: Uint<8>,
  winner: Uint<8>,
  turn: Uint<16>,
  paid: Uint<64>,
  rake: Uint<64>,
  board: Board,
}

pure circuit finalDigestOf(
               id: Bytes<32>,
               ending: Uint<8>,
               win: Uint<8>,
               plies: Uint<16>,
               paid: Uint<64>,
               rake: Uint<64>,
               b: Board,
               ): Bytes<32> {
  return persistentHash<BgFinalEvent>(
           BgFinalEvent { domain: pad(32, "dust-dice:bg:v3:final"),
                          tableId: id,
                          ending: ending,
                          winner: win,
                          turn: plies,
                          paid: paid,
                          rake: rake,
                          board: b, }
           );
}

/** Rake split: `q * 100 + r == pot`, `r < 100`. Returns `[winnerPayout, rake]`. */
pure circuit bgSplitPot(total: Uint<64>, q: Uint<64>, r: Uint<64>): Vector<2, Uint<64>> {
  assert((q * 100 + r == total) && (r < 100),
         "rake split must satisfy q*100 + r == pot with r < 100"
         );
  return [(total - q) as Uint<64>, q];
}

/** Must be the last thing every exported circuit does. See table.compact's `padTransaction`. */
circuit padTransaction(): [] {
  kernel.checkpoint();
  padStore = paddingConstant();
}

/** Pin a declared (already disclosed) `now` to block time. See table.compact's `pinTime`. */
circuit pinTime(now: Uint<64>): [] {
  assert(blockTimeGte(now), "declared time is ahead of block time");
  assert(blockTimeLt((now + timeSlackSecs()) as Uint<64>),
         "declared time is further behind block time than the allowed slack"
         );
}

/** The deadline a call starts for the seat it hands the game to: a slack ahead (section 3). */
circuit clockFrom(now: Uint<64>): Uint<64> {
  return (now + timeSlackSecs() + moveTimeoutSecs) as Uint<64>;
}

/** Every stake received is still in the pot: nothing leaves before the end. */
circuit assertCustody(): [] {
  assert(pot == (tier * (seatCount as Uint<64>)) as Uint<64>,
         "custody invariant broken: pot != tier * seatCount"
         );
}

/** Prove knowledge of seat `s`'s secret. */
circuit proveSeat(s: Uint<8>): Bytes<32> {
  const sk = playerEntropySecret();
  assert(disclose(entropyKeyCommitment(tableId, sk) == seatIdentity.lookup(s).keyCommit),
         "wrong entropy secret for this seat"
         );
  return sk;
}

/** Has seat `s` revealed its share of the opening? A forced share is zeros only by accident. */
circuit hasShare(s: Uint<8>): Boolean {
  return openingShare.lookup(s) != default<Bytes<32>>;
}

/** The seat the opening makes move first: the higher of the two opening dice. */
circuit openingStarter(): Uint<8> {
  const od = bgOpeningDice(tableId, openingShare.lookup(0), openingShare.lookup(1));
  return od.a > od.b ? 0 : 1;
}

/** One slot, as the start and the filling abort read it. */
export struct SlotInfo {
  slot: Uint<8>,
  occupied: Boolean,
  joinedAt: Uint<64>,
  addr: UserAddress,
}

/** A free slot: the zero address marks it free (a join refuses a zero payout address). */
pure circuit emptySlot(): BgSlot {
  return BgSlot { addr: default<UserAddress>, keyCommit: default<Bytes<32>>, joinedAt: 0 };
}

/** A slot's holder, as a seat identity. */
circuit slotSeat(i: Uint<8>): SeatIdentity {
  const sl = joinSlots.lookup(i);
  return SeatIdentity { addr: sl.addr, keyCommit: sl.keyCommit };
}

circuit slotInfo(i: Uint<8>): SlotInfo {
  const id = joinSlots.lookup(i);
  return SlotInfo { slot: i,
                    occupied: id.addr != default<UserAddress>,
                    joinedAt: id.joinedAt,
                    addr: id.addr, };
}

/** Every slot. Read only by the start and the filling abort -- never by a join. */
circuit allSlots(): Vector<8, SlotInfo> {
  return [slotInfo(0), slotInfo(1), slotInfo(2), slotInfo(3),
          slotInfo(4), slotInfo(5), slotInfo(6), slotInfo(7)];
}

/** The two earliest joiners, how many joined, and when the last did. */
export struct Pick2 {
  count: Uint<8>,
  a: Uint<8>,
  aKey: Uint<64>,
  b: Uint<8>,
  bKey: Uint<64>,
  latest: Uint<64>,
}

pure circuit noPick2(): Pick2 {
  return Pick2 { count: 0, a: 0, aKey: 18446744073709551615, b: 0, bKey: 18446744073709551615,
                 latest: 0 };
}

/** Earlier declared join first; the slot index breaks a tie, so the order is total. */
pure circuit pickTwo(acc: Pick2, s: SlotInfo): Pick2 {
  const key = (s.joinedAt * 16 + s.slot) as Uint<64>;
  const beforeA = key < acc.aKey;
  const beforeB = key < acc.bKey;
  return !s.occupied
           ? acc
           : Pick2 { count: (acc.count + 1) as Uint<8>,
                     a: beforeA ? s.slot : acc.a,
                     aKey: beforeA ? key : acc.aKey,
                     b: beforeA ? acc.a : (beforeB ? s.slot : acc.b),
                     bKey: beforeA ? acc.aKey : (beforeB ? key : acc.bKey),
                     latest: s.joinedAt > acc.latest ? s.joinedAt : acc.latest, };
}

/** Refund every occupied slot, except the two seated ones when `keep`. */
circuit refundSlots(slots: Vector<8, SlotInfo>, keepA: Uint<8>, keepB: Uint<8>, keep: Boolean): [] {
  for (const s of slots) {
    if (s.occupied && !(keep && (s.slot == keepA || s.slot == keepB))) {
      sendUnshielded(nativeToken(), tier as Uint<128>, right<ContractAddress, UserAddress>(s.addr));
    }
  }
}

// ===========================================================================================
// Constructor
// ===========================================================================================

constructor(id: Bytes<32>,
            stake: Uint<64>,
            rakeTo: UserAddress,
            moveTimeout: Uint<64>,
            tableTimeout: Uint<64>,
            invite: Bytes<32>,
            ) {
  assert(stake >= minTier(), "tier must be at least 100 so the 1% rake is non-zero");
  assert(stake <= maxTier(), "tier is above the maximum a table may stake");
  const floor = (timeSlackSecs() * timeoutSlackFactor()) as Uint<64>;
  assert(moveTimeout >= minMoveSecs(), "move timeout must be at least two minutes");
  assert(tableTimeout > floor, "table timeout must exceed the declared-time floor");

  tableId = disclose(id);
  tier = disclose(stake);
  rakeAddress = disclose(rakeTo);
  moveTimeoutSecs = disclose(moveTimeout);
  tableTimeoutSecs = disclose(tableTimeout);
  inviteHash = disclose(invite);

  phase = BgPhase.filling;
  seatCount = 0;
  toMove = 0;
  stage = stageOpening();
  turn = 0;
  dice = BgDice { a: 0, b: 0 };
  board = Board { s0: bgInitialSide(), s1: bgInitialSide() };
  lastPly = default<Ply>;
  // Stays 0 until the start: a filling table's clock is its latest join (see `abortTable`).
  deadline = 0;
  pot = 0;
  winner = noWinner();
  finalDigest = default<Bytes<32>>;
  lastSecret = default<Bytes<32>>;

  // Both seats pre-inserted, so every lookup at a fixed index is total (table.compact section 7).
  for (const s of 0..2) {
    seatIdentity.insert(
      s as Uint<8>,
      SeatIdentity { addr: default<UserAddress>, keyCommit: default<Bytes<32>> }
      );
    openingShare.insert(s as Uint<8>, default<Bytes<32>>);
  }
  // Every slot pre-inserted: a join then overwrites one cell of a fixed size, so its cost does
  // not depend on how many others joined first (the gas trap of docs/order-independent-join.md).
  //
  // ONE MAP OF RECORDS, NOT A MAP PER FIELD: each map entry carries its own overhead, and with a
  // slot's fields in four maps of eight the deploy was refused ("Transaction would exhaust the
  // block limits", devnet 2026-09-29: 32,345 bytes written against the Yacht table's 28,920,
  // which lands). It also lets the start and the filling abort read eight cells, not sixteen.
  for (const s of 0..8) {
    joinSlots.insert(s as Uint<8>, emptySlot());
  }
}

// ===========================================================================================
// join
// ===========================================================================================

/**
 * Claim `slot`, stake `tier` and commit to the seat key. Nothing else is revealed: the opening's
 * share comes after the start (backgammon-dice.compact), so no joiner can tune its key to it.
 *
 * Reads `phase`, constants and this slot, and nothing else (section 6), so joins to different
 * slots land together. The game starts with `start`, not with a join: a join cannot know it is
 * the second without reading what the other joins wrote.
 */
export circuit join(slot: Uint<8>, payoutTo: UserAddress, now: Uint<64>): Uint<8> {
  assert(phase == BgPhase.filling, "join: table is not filling");

  const s = disclose(slot);
  const t = disclose(now);
  const who = disclose(payoutTo);
  assert(s < slotCount(), "join: no such seat");
  pinTime(t);
  assert(who != default<UserAddress>, "join: payout address must not be the zero address");
  // THE GUARD: two joins to one slot both read it, so exactly one lands.
  assert(joinSlots.lookup(s).addr == default<UserAddress>,
         "join: that seat was taken a moment ago -- pick another"
         );

  const sk = playerEntropySecret();
  const commit = disclose(entropyKeyCommitment(tableId, sk));

  const code = inviteCode();
  assert(disclose(inviteHash == default<Bytes<32>> || inviteCommitment(code) == inviteHash),
         "join: this table is private -- the invite code does not match"
         );

  joinSlots.insert(s, BgSlot { addr: who, keyCommit: commit, joinedAt: t });
  receiveUnshielded(nativeToken(), tier as Uint<128>);

  padTransaction();
  return s;
}

// ===========================================================================================
// start -- seat the players
// ===========================================================================================

/**
 * The START (section 6): seat the two earliest joiners, refund anyone else who joined in the same
 * moment, set the pot, and start both seats' clocks for their opening shares. Anyone may send
 * it -- it takes no secret and decides nothing a caller could steer.
 */
export circuit start(now: Uint<64>): [] {
  assert(phase == BgPhase.filling, "start: table is not filling");

  const t = disclose(now);
  pinTime(t);

  const slots = allSlots();
  const p = fold(pickTwo, noPick2(), slots);
  assert(p.count >= 2, "start: fewer than two players have joined");
  seatIdentity.insert(0, slotSeat(p.a));
  seatIdentity.insert(1, slotSeat(p.b));
  refundSlots(slots, p.a, p.b, true);
  pot = (tier * 2) as Uint<64>;
  seatCount = 2;
  phase = BgPhase.playing;
  stage = stageOpening();
  deadline = clockFrom(t);

  padTransaction();
}

// ===========================================================================================
// reveal -- a seat's share of the opening
// ===========================================================================================

/**
 * Reveal seat `seat`'s share of the opening, forced from its key (backgammon-dice.compact).
 *
 * Reads the phase, the stage, this seat's identity and this seat's own share cell -- nothing the
 * other seat's reveal writes -- so both reveals can land in one block. `deadline` is written
 * blind: the later reveal's stamp stands, and the starter's clock runs from the second share.
 */
export circuit reveal(seat: Uint<8>, now: Uint<64>): [] {
  assert(phase == BgPhase.playing, "reveal: table is not playing");
  assert(stage == stageOpening(), "reveal: the opening has been played");

  const s = disclose(seat);
  const t = disclose(now);
  assert(s < 2, "reveal: no such seat");
  pinTime(t);

  const sk = proveSeat(s);
  assert(!hasShare(s), "reveal: this seat has revealed its share already");
  openingShare.insert(s, disclose(bgOpeningSecret(sk, tableId)));
  deadline = clockFrom(t);

  padTransaction();
}

// ===========================================================================================
// openingMove and move -- the player to move
// ===========================================================================================

/**
 * Play a ply with dice `d` for `mover`: up to four sub-moves, in order, each checked against the
 * board as it stands when that checker moves; `count == 0` is a pass (no legal move -- see
 * section 1). Records the ply, discloses its dice and the ply's secret `v` (the other seat's next
 * roll hashes it), and hands the game on -- or, when the mover bore off its fifteenth checker,
 * decides it.
 */
circuit playPly(mover: Uint<8>,
                d: BgDice,
                p: Vector<4, SubMove>,
                c: Uint<8>,
                v: Bytes<32>,
                n: Uint<16>,
                t: Uint<64>,
                ): Uint<16> {
  const bd = board;
  const first = mover == 0;
  const out = applyPlyStrict(first ? bd.s0 : bd.s1, first ? bd.s1 : bd.s0, d.a, d.b, p, c);

  board = first ? Board { s0: out.mine, s1: out.opp } : Board { s0: out.opp, s1: out.mine };
  lastPly = Ply { moves: p, count: c };
  dice = d;
  lastSecret = disclose(v);
  const next = (n + 1) as Uint<16>;
  turn = next;
  stage = stageMove();

  if (out.won) {
    phase = BgPhase.decided;
    winner = mover;
  } else {
    toMove = (1 - mover) as Uint<8>;
    deadline = clockFrom(t);
  }
  return next;
}

/**
 * Ply 0: played by the higher opening die's seat, with the opening pair -- both read from the two
 * shares. A circuit of its own so that `move`, which every other ply proves, does not also pay
 * for the opening's hash: that put `move` at k=16 rather than 15 (measured 2026-09-29).
 */
export circuit openingMove(ply: Vector<4, SubMove>, count: Uint<8>, now: Uint<64>): Uint<16> {
  assert(phase == BgPhase.playing, "openingMove: table is not playing");
  assert(turn == 0, "openingMove: the opening has been played");
  assert(hasShare(0) && hasShare(1),
         "openingMove: the opening is not thrown yet -- both shares are needed"
         );

  const t = disclose(now);
  pinTime(t);

  const od = bgOpeningDice(tableId, openingShare.lookup(0), openingShare.lookup(1));
  const mover = (od.a > od.b ? 0 : 1) as Uint<8>;
  const sk = proveSeat(mover);
  const v = bgRollSecret(sk, tableId, 0);
  // The opening pair is public already (both shares are on the ledger); this discloses no more.
  const next = playPly(mover, disclose(od), disclose(ply), disclose(count), v, 0, t);

  padTransaction();
  return next;
}

/**
 * Ply 1 on. The dice hash the mover's secret for this ply, revealed here, and the secret the
 * previous move revealed (backgammon-dice.compact).
 */
export circuit move(ply: Vector<4, SubMove>, count: Uint<8>, now: Uint<64>): Uint<16> {
  assert(phase == BgPhase.playing, "move: table is not playing");
  const n = turn;
  assert(n > 0, "move: ply 0 is the opening's -- openingMove plays it");

  const t = disclose(now);
  pinTime(t);

  const mover = toMove;
  const sk = proveSeat(mover);
  const v = bgRollSecret(sk, tableId, n);
  // The one disclosure of the roll: the dice are public from this move on.
  const d = disclose(bgPlyDice(tableId, v, lastSecret, n));
  const next = playPly(mover, d, disclose(ply), disclose(count), v, n, t);

  padTransaction();
  return next;
}

// ===========================================================================================
// eliminate -- a timeout, a resignation, or leaving a filling table
// ===========================================================================================

/**
 * Three things, by state:
 *
 *   * playing, not voluntary: the seat that owes, past the deadline -> the opponent wins.
 *     Permissionless. The seat that owes is the one to move -- or, at the opening, a seat whose
 *     share is missing while the other's is in, or the starter once both are in.
 *   * playing, voluntary: the seat resigns (proves its secret) -> the opponent wins.
 *   * filling, voluntary: the seat leaves its slot and is refunded in full.
 */
export circuit eliminate(seat: Uint<8>, voluntary: Boolean): Uint<8> {
  const s = disclose(seat);
  const vol = disclose(voluntary);
  const filling = phase == BgPhase.filling;
  const playing = phase == BgPhase.playing;
  // While filling, `seat` names a SLOT (leaving it); in play, seat 0 or 1.
  assert(filling ? s < slotCount() : s < seatCount, "eliminate: no such seat");

  const sk = playerEntropySecret();
  const who = filling ? slotSeat(s < slotCount() ? s : 0)
                      : seatIdentity.lookup(s < 2 ? s : 0);
  assert(!filling || who.addr != default<UserAddress>, "eliminate: that seat is empty");
  assert(disclose(!vol || entropyKeyCommitment(tableId, sk) == who.keyCommit),
         "eliminate: wrong entropy secret for this seat"
         );

  // Who owes right now. At the opening: a missing share while the other's is in, or -- both in --
  // the starter's ply 0. Neither share in is nobody's fault alone: `abortTable` refunds it.
  const other = (1 - (s < 2 ? s : 0)) as Uint<8>;
  const opening = stage == stageOpening();
  const mine = playing && opening && hasShare(s < 2 ? s : 0);
  const theirs = playing && opening && hasShare(other);
  const owes = opening
                 ? ((!mine && theirs) || (mine && theirs && openingStarter() == s))
                 : s == toMove;
  const timedOut = playing && !vol && owes && blockTimeGt(deadline);
  assert((filling && vol) || (playing && vol) || timedOut,
         "eliminate: this seat is not past its deadline, and is not leaving of its own accord"
         );

  if (filling) {
    // Leaving empties the slot for someone else, and reads nothing another join writes.
    sendUnshielded(nativeToken(), tier as Uint<128>, right<ContractAddress, UserAddress>(who.addr));
    joinSlots.insert(s, emptySlot());
  } else {
    winner = other;
    phase = BgPhase.decided;
  }

  padTransaction();
  return s;
}

// ===========================================================================================
// settle -- pay the winner
// ===========================================================================================

/**
 * Pay the winner the pot less the 1% rake. Permissionless: every input is on the ledger, and
 * nothing is left to reveal -- every roll was proven by the move that played it, and replays from
 * the secrets the moves revealed.
 */
export circuit settle(q: Uint<64>, r: Uint<64>): Uint<8> {
  assert(phase == BgPhase.decided, "settle: the game is not decided");

  assertCustody();

  const w = winner;
  const split = bgSplitPot(pot, disclose(q), disclose(r));
  sendUnshielded(
    nativeToken(),
    split[0] as Uint<128>,
    right<ContractAddress, UserAddress>(seatIdentity.lookup(w).addr)
    );
  sendUnshielded(
    nativeToken(),
    split[1] as Uint<128>,
    right<ContractAddress, UserAddress>(rakeAddress)
    );

  pot = 0;
  finalDigest =
    finalDigestOf(tableId, BgPhase.settled as Uint<8>, w, turn, split[0], split[1], board);
  phase = BgPhase.settled;

  padTransaction();
  return w;
}

// ===========================================================================================
// abortTable -- refund a table that cannot finish
// ===========================================================================================

/**
 * Permissionless. Refunds every stake in full, no rake, in two situations:
 *
 *   * filling, and nobody new has joined for a table timeout (a lone player waiting, or a full
 *     table nobody started);
 *   * the opening, NEITHER seat has revealed its share, and a table timeout has passed on top of
 *     the deadline. One share in and the other missing is the missing seat's fault: `eliminate`.
 */
export circuit abortTable(): Uint<64> {
  const filling = phase == BgPhase.filling;
  const slots = allSlots();
  const p = fold(pickTwo, noPick2(), slots);
  const fillingStalled =
          filling && p.count > 0 && blockTimeGt((p.latest + tableTimeoutSecs) as Uint<64>);
  const openingStalled =
          phase == BgPhase.playing &&
          stage == stageOpening() &&
          !hasShare(0) &&
          !hasShare(1) &&
          blockTimeGt((deadline + tableTimeoutSecs) as Uint<64>);
  assert(fillingStalled || openingStalled,
         "abortTable: the table is not stalled past its deadline"
         );

  // While filling, nothing counted the stakes (section 6): the slots are the account of them.
  // After the start the seated players are still in their slots, so those are refunded as SEATS
  // and the slots are not touched -- refunding both would pay them twice.
  assertCustody();
  if (filling) {
    refundSlots(slots, 0, 0, false);
  } else {
    sendUnshielded(
      nativeToken(),
      tier as Uint<128>,
      right<ContractAddress, UserAddress>(seatIdentity.lookup(0).addr)
      );
    sendUnshielded(
      nativeToken(),
      tier as Uint<128>,
      right<ContractAddress, UserAddress>(seatIdentity.lookup(1).addr)
      );
  }

  const refunded = filling ? (tier * (p.count as Uint<64>)) as Uint<64> : pot;
  pot = 0;
  finalDigest = finalDigestOf(tableId, BgPhase.aborted as Uint<8>, noWinner(), turn, 0, 0, board);
  phase = BgPhase.aborted;

  padTransaction();
  return refunded;
}

export { UserAddress, ContractAddress };
